summary:
  A stop sends SIGINT whatever state the process is in, also to an ffmpeg
  that has not set its signal handlers yet and ignores it.

history:
  A shell's background job ignores SIGINT until it installs handlers of its
  own. Measured 2026-11-10: a stop sent as the pid file appeared found ffmpeg
  with SigIgn 0x6 and nothing caught, the signal was lost and the capture ran
  on; 50 ms later the same stop worked. A process that still ignores SIGINT
  is now ended with SIGTERM, which it does not ignore.

target:
  package/contents/ui/RecordingEngine.qml

find:
              + " then kill -TERM \"$p\"; else kill -INT \"$p\"; fi; } 2>/dev/null; true");

replace:
              + " then kill -INT \"$p\"; else kill -INT \"$p\"; fi; } 2>/dev/null; true");

expect:
  Killed by tests/qml/tst_recordingengine.qml::test_a_stop_sends_the_signal_the_process_can_hear
  and tests/test_recording_stop_race.py::test_a_stop_the_moment_the_pid_is_written_ends_the_capture.
  Line-neutral.
